1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,840
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,860 --> 00:00:09,401
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:09,801 --> 00:00:16,221
This is Lecture 14, the control of the industrial lockstitch machine.

4
00:00:16,621 --> 00:00:19,723
Last time we met servo motors and solenoids.

5
00:00:19,743 --> 00:00:23,058
Today we put them together with the mechanisms,

6
00:00:23,078 --> 00:00:25,470
and see how the machine backtacks,

7
00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:30,792
trims and wipes automatically by shaft angle. One idea runs through it all.

8
00:00:30,812 --> 00:00:35,121
Every action has to line up with the mechanical timing chart.

9
00:00:36,641 --> 00:00:39,741
Here is the shop-floor problem.

10
00:00:40,141 --> 00:00:48,034
On a shirt floor the electronically controlled lockstitch machines sew at

11
00:00:48,054 --> 00:00:49,226
4000 r/min,

12
00:00:49,246 --> 00:00:54,321
and each operator trims about 3000 times a day.

13
00:00:54,721 --> 00:01:03,981
The engineer wants to raise the trimming speed from 300 to 500 r/min.

14
00:01:04,381 --> 00:01:09,607
The trimming solenoid has a 6 to 10 millisecond delay,

15
00:01:09,627 --> 00:01:14,367
the knife must enter between 300 and 330 degrees,

16
00:01:14,387 --> 00:01:20,001
and after the cut the shaft must stop before 96.7 degrees.

17
00:01:20,401 --> 00:01:23,052
How much time does it save?

18
00:01:23,452 --> 00:01:25,232
Will it still cut every time?

19
00:01:25,632 --> 00:01:27,692
What command angle should we use?

20
00:01:28,092 --> 00:01:29,432
We'll answer at the end.

21
00:01:30,952 --> 00:01:33,892
One working cycle: a state machine.

22
00:01:35,412 --> 00:01:40,429
At the end of a seam the operator presses back with the heel,

23
00:01:40,449 --> 00:01:45,300
and within half a second the machine backtacks, slows down,

24
00:01:45,320 --> 00:01:50,832
sews one trimming stitch, cuts and stops, wipes and lifts the foot.

25
00:01:51,232 --> 00:01:56,314
A forward press drops the foot, backtacks, starts slowly,

26
00:01:56,334 --> 00:01:58,372
then follows the pedal.

27
00:01:58,772 --> 00:02:03,846
The controller runs this as a state machine: boxes are states,

28
00:02:03,866 --> 00:02:08,529
arrows are triggers such as pedal position, stitch count,

29
00:02:08,549 --> 00:02:12,472
shaft angle or an actuator's in-position signal.

30
00:02:12,872 --> 00:02:20,972
Every step is explicit, so if one hangs, the controller reports exactly which.

31
00:02:22,492 --> 00:02:27,404
Let's account for that half-second.

32
00:02:27,804 --> 00:02:35,016
Stopping and trimming from 4000 r/min has three parts.

33
00:02:35,416 --> 00:02:42,492
First, decelerate to 300 r/min at 60% of the servo's capability,

34
00:02:42,512 --> 00:02:48,701
about 2650 radians per second squared: 146 milliseconds.

35
00:02:49,101 --> 00:02:51,980
Then the trimming stitch,

36
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:59,867
from 0° all the way to the cut at 55° of the next turn, 415° in all:

37
00:02:59,887 --> 00:03:04,043
231 milliseconds, the biggest share.

38
00:03:04,443 --> 00:03:09,783
Finally a stop at maximum deceleration: just 7 milliseconds.

39
00:03:10,183 --> 00:03:18,645
About 0.38 seconds; add wiping and foot lift and it is roughly half a second.

40
00:03:20,165 --> 00:03:24,078
Automatic backtack: reverse inside the idle angle.

41
00:03:25,598 --> 00:03:31,818
A few reverse stitches at the start and end lock the seam: that's backtacking.

42
00:03:32,218 --> 00:03:38,479
Mechanical feed uses a reverse-feed solenoid to shift the stitch regulator;

43
00:03:38,499 --> 00:03:42,163
electronic feed reverses a stepper directly.

44
00:03:42,563 --> 00:03:44,498
Timing is everything.

45
00:03:44,898 --> 00:03:49,040
While the feed dog is above the plate it is pushing the fabric,

46
00:03:49,060 --> 00:03:53,533
and a reversal then gives a stitch that is neither forward nor back.

47
00:03:53,933 --> 00:04:05,105
In the Lecture 6 example the dog drops at 114.2° and rises at 325.8°;

48
00:04:05,125 --> 00:04:11,593
the 211.6° in between is the idle angle.

49
00:04:11,993 --> 00:04:19,069
Command at 114.2°, the shaft turns 6 n t degrees during delay t,

50
00:04:19,089 --> 00:04:22,285
and we keep 10 degrees spare.

51
00:04:23,805 --> 00:04:29,279
Take the idle angle, subtract the 10-degree margin and divide by 6t:

52
00:04:29,299 --> 00:04:31,865
that's the backtack speed limit.

53
00:04:32,265 --> 00:04:41,562
A reverse-feed solenoid has to push links and a spring, so its delay is long:

54
00:04:41,582 --> 00:04:47,491
at 18 milliseconds the limit is about 1867 r/min,

55
00:04:47,511 --> 00:04:51,121
at 20 milliseconds about 1680.

56
00:04:51,521 --> 00:05:01,662
A feed stepper reverses in 5 milliseconds, which allows 6720 r/min,

57
00:05:01,682 --> 00:05:07,121
faster than the sewing speed itself.

58
00:05:07,521 --> 00:05:09,861
Delay is no longer the limit.

59
00:05:11,381 --> 00:05:13,801
Trimming: on the timing chart.

60
00:05:15,321 --> 00:05:17,141
Here is one trim.

61
00:05:17,541 --> 00:05:23,152
Under the throat plate there is a fixed knife and a moving knife;

62
00:05:23,172 --> 00:05:28,610
here a solenoid drives the moving knife directly through links.

63
00:05:29,010 --> 00:05:32,601
The last stitch is sewn as usual,

64
00:05:32,621 --> 00:05:38,950
and the hook point catches the needle-thread loop at 206°.

65
00:05:39,350 --> 00:05:45,826
When the needle has risen about 10 millimetres above the plate,

66
00:05:45,846 --> 00:05:53,250
about 291° in our model, the controller energises the trimming solenoid.

67
00:05:53,650 --> 00:05:59,260
After the delay the knife swings in under the supply side of the loop,

68
00:05:59,280 --> 00:06:05,373
and the fabric-side needle thread and the bobbin thread fall into its notch.

69
00:06:05,773 --> 00:06:12,953
At 341° the loop slips off and the tension opens.

70
00:06:13,353 --> 00:06:20,877
On its return the knife cuts both threads at the take-up lever's top,

71
00:06:20,897 --> 00:06:23,173
55° of the next turn.

72
00:06:23,573 --> 00:06:28,953
The supply-side thread stays in the eye for the next seam.

73
00:06:30,473 --> 00:06:35,339
Put the trim on the Lecture 8 timing chart and four criteria appear;

74
00:06:35,359 --> 00:06:38,573
the values are this book's illustrative ones.

75
00:06:38,973 --> 00:06:46,195
Here is the lab's timing chart: green is the window where the knife may enter,

76
00:06:46,215 --> 00:06:51,117
red the actual entry range once the delay is counted.

77
00:06:51,517 --> 00:06:53,777
Watch the command angle change.

78
00:06:54,177 --> 00:06:58,730
Too early, and the loop hasn't reached the knife's path,

79
00:06:58,750 --> 00:07:01,424
so only the bobbin thread is cut.

80
00:07:01,824 --> 00:07:06,044
Too late, and the loop has already slipped off.

81
00:07:06,444 --> 00:07:12,377
Also: after the cut at 55°, stop before 96.7°;

82
00:07:12,397 --> 00:07:21,824
keep the tension open from at least 335° to 55°; and wipe after stopping.

83
00:07:23,344 --> 00:07:27,985
The controller commands at a chosen command angle,

84
00:07:28,005 --> 00:07:33,298
and during delay t the shaft turns another 6 n t degrees,

85
00:07:33,318 --> 00:07:38,984
so the knife actually enters at the command angle plus 6 n t.

86
00:07:39,384 --> 00:07:48,144
Here the command stays at 290° with a 6 to 10 millisecond delay.

87
00:07:48,544 --> 00:08:00,226
At 300 r/min the knife enters between 300.8° and 308°, inside the window.

88
00:08:00,626 --> 00:08:11,379
As the speed steps up to 700, the red bar moves right to 315.2° to 332°:

89
00:08:11,399 --> 00:08:12,726
too late.

90
00:08:13,126 --> 00:08:17,586
The command would have to move before 288°.

91
00:08:17,986 --> 00:08:24,683
Notice too that the red bar widens, from 7 to 17 degrees.

92
00:08:26,203 --> 00:08:29,224
The average delay is not the problem:

93
00:08:29,244 --> 00:08:35,223
the command angle can be advanced automatically for speed and mean delay.

94
00:08:35,623 --> 00:08:38,655
The problem is that the delay varies;

95
00:08:38,675 --> 00:08:42,203
on each trim it falls somewhere in a range.

96
00:08:42,603 --> 00:08:50,396
Both sides run at 500 r/min with the command at 291°,

97
00:08:50,416 --> 00:08:59,683
trying 50 cuts over and over; green is a clean cut, red a miss.

98
00:09:00,083 --> 00:09:04,663
On the left the delay is 6 to 10 milliseconds;

99
00:09:04,683 --> 00:09:11,863
on the right low voltage and a worn spring have stretched it to 5 to 15.

100
00:09:12,263 --> 00:09:14,563
Compare the red on the two sides.

101
00:09:16,083 --> 00:09:16,676
Two limits.

102
00:09:17,076 --> 00:09:19,207
The first is the window:

103
00:09:19,227 --> 00:09:26,466
the entry range is 6n times the delay spread wide and must fit inside 30 degrees.

104
00:09:26,866 --> 00:09:38,566
With 6 to 10 milliseconds that allows 1250 r/min; with 5 to 15, only 500.

105
00:09:38,966 --> 00:09:47,868
The second is stopping: cut at 55°, stop before 96.7°, so within 41.7°;

106
00:09:47,888 --> 00:09:56,538
at the maximum deceleration of about 4417 radians per second squared,

107
00:09:56,558 --> 00:10:02,066
trimming speed can't exceed about 766 r/min.

108
00:10:02,466 --> 00:10:05,565
Take the lower of the two.

109
00:10:05,965 --> 00:10:09,091
That's why a regulated supply,

110
00:10:09,111 --> 00:10:17,376
boost drive and Zener freewheeling matter most for making the delay consistent.

111
00:10:18,896 --> 00:10:24,176
Worked example: trimming at 500 r/min.

112
00:10:25,696 --> 00:10:28,720
Back to the problem: the command angle.

113
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:35,766
Even with the shortest delay the knife must not enter before 300°,

114
00:10:35,786 --> 00:10:42,128
so the angle plus 6 times 500 times 0.006 must be at least 300:

115
00:10:42,148 --> 00:10:44,552
the angle at least 282°.

116
00:10:44,952 --> 00:10:52,008
Even with the longest delay it must not enter after 330°,

117
00:10:52,028 --> 00:10:59,704
so the angle plus 6 times 500 times 0.010 must be at most 330:

118
00:10:59,724 --> 00:11:02,559
the angle at most 300°.

119
00:11:02,959 --> 00:11:08,979
Take the middle, about 291°, leaving a margin at both ends.

120
00:11:09,379 --> 00:11:14,919
The three bars show the actual entry range for each of these command angles.

121
00:11:16,439 --> 00:11:21,379
A robot picking parts off a conveyor faces the same problem as trimming.

122
00:11:21,779 --> 00:11:25,113
The gripper's solenoid valve has a delay,

123
00:11:25,133 --> 00:11:29,775
so the controller commands early according to belt speed,

124
00:11:29,795 --> 00:11:34,519
just as the trimming command angle moves with shaft speed.

125
00:11:34,919 --> 00:11:37,453
The mean delay can be compensated,

126
00:11:37,473 --> 00:11:43,238
but its spread scatters the grip point over the belt speed times that spread,

127
00:11:43,258 --> 00:11:46,093
which has to fit the gripper's window,

128
00:11:46,113 --> 00:11:49,474
so the belt speed limit is set by the spread.

129
00:11:49,874 --> 00:11:56,611
The cell's pick, carry, place and return are sequenced by a state machine too,

130
00:11:56,631 --> 00:12:02,934
and a robot senses contact force through joint current or a force sensor.

131
00:12:04,454 --> 00:12:05,937
In everyday life,

132
00:12:05,957 --> 00:12:11,951
the closest thing to trimming timing is the baton change in a relay.

133
00:12:12,351 --> 00:12:16,756
The receiver must take the baton inside the changeover zone,

134
00:12:16,776 --> 00:12:20,885
so they start running when their teammate passes a mark:

135
00:12:20,905 --> 00:12:23,171
that's the early command angle.

136
00:12:23,571 --> 00:12:28,064
The faster the teammate, the further back the mark,

137
00:12:28,084 --> 00:12:32,311
just as a faster shaft needs an earlier command.

138
00:12:32,711 --> 00:12:38,904
Coaches know most failed changes come not from a slow reaction but from an

139
00:12:38,924 --> 00:12:40,331
inconsistent one.

140
00:12:40,731 --> 00:12:46,551
Same as trimming: the spread of the delay hurts more than the delay itself.

141
00:12:48,071 --> 00:12:49,771
Back to the opening question.

142
00:12:50,171 --> 00:12:59,350
Raising the trimming speed from 300 to 500 cuts one stop-and-trim from 0.38

143
00:12:59,370 --> 00:13:01,312
to 0.29 seconds,

144
00:13:01,332 --> 00:13:04,255
about 4.8 minutes a day.

145
00:13:04,655 --> 00:13:13,786
With a 6 to 10 millisecond delay the window limit is 1250 r/min and the

146
00:13:13,806 --> 00:13:17,008
stopping limit about 766,

147
00:13:17,028 --> 00:13:19,843
so 500 is within both.

148
00:13:20,243 --> 00:13:31,835
The command angle can be 282° to 300°; take 291°.

149
00:13:32,235 --> 00:13:39,666
One warning: if low voltage stretches the delay to 5 to 15 milliseconds,

150
00:13:39,686 --> 00:13:45,255
the window limit drops to exactly 500, with no margin.

151
00:13:45,655 --> 00:13:49,115
So steady the delay before you speed up.

152
00:13:50,635 --> 00:13:54,151
Now Virtual lab 14-1, the trimming sequence editor.

153
00:13:54,171 --> 00:13:58,450
On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself. The verdicts,

154
00:13:58,470 --> 00:14:02,194
the hints and the tasks on the right are blurred here,

155
00:14:02,214 --> 00:14:06,563
so you can read them on your own. Top left is the timing chart.

156
00:14:06,583 --> 00:14:09,752
Green is the window where the knife may enter,

157
00:14:09,772 --> 00:14:13,912
and red is the actual entry range once the delay is counted.

158
00:14:14,312 --> 00:14:16,291
Press Another 50 trials.

159
00:14:16,311 --> 00:14:21,038
Each delay falls at random between 6 and 10 milliseconds,

160
00:14:21,058 --> 00:14:26,369
and the row of cells at the bottom left shows the 50 trial cuts.

161
00:14:26,769 --> 00:14:30,868
Task one: press Scramble. The command jumps to 250 degrees,

162
00:14:30,888 --> 00:14:34,988
the release range gets shorter, and wiping is switched off.

163
00:14:35,008 --> 00:14:40,015
Here we drag the command angle a little later and watch the red bar move

164
00:14:40,035 --> 00:14:41,272
toward the window.

165
00:14:41,292 --> 00:14:46,439
The rest of the command angle, the release and the wiper are yours to fix,

166
00:14:46,459 --> 00:14:48,324
until every verdict passes.

167
00:14:48,724 --> 00:14:49,858
Task two: reset,

168
00:14:49,878 --> 00:14:54,475
and raise the trimming speed to 700 or more with the same delay.

169
00:14:54,495 --> 00:14:58,730
The red bar moves right, out of the window, and gets wider.

170
00:14:58,750 --> 00:15:03,707
The slides gave the upper end; work out how early the command may go,

171
00:15:03,727 --> 00:15:07,602
then check it with the slider. All 50 trials must cut.

172
00:15:08,002 --> 00:15:13,749
Task three: set the delay to 5 to 15 milliseconds. The red bar widens at once,

173
00:15:13,769 --> 00:15:16,706
and some of the 50 trials start to fail.

174
00:15:16,726 --> 00:15:22,251
The slides worked out the window limit; compare it with the stopping limit,

175
00:15:22,271 --> 00:15:27,500
enter the result to check, then confirm with the slider and the trials.

